{"word": "humour", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "humour", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈhjuː.mə(ɹ)/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈhjuːmɚ/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈjuːmɚ/", "tags": ["General American"]}], "forms": [{"word": "humours", "tags": ["plural"]}, {"word": "humor", "tags": ["alternative", "US"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(chiefly uncountable) The quality of being amusing, comical, funny.", "tags": ["UK", "uncountable", "usually"], "examples": ["She has a great sense of humour, and I always laugh a lot whenever we get together.", "The sensitive subject was treated with humour, but in such way that no one was offended."], "quotes": [{"text": "\"I gathered that you might not be altogether sorry to see me. So\"—with a reassuring laugh—\"here I am.\" A mild amusement at the possible humours of the situation characterised his manner.", "reference": "1934, Ernest Bramah, The Bravo of London:"}, {"text": "Merry VVhitefoord, farevvel! for thy ſake I admit / That a Scot may have humour, I had almoſt ſaid vvit: […]", "reference": "1774, [Oliver] Goldsmith, “Postscript”, in Retaliation: A Poem. […], 5th edition, London: […] G[eorge] Kearsly, […], →OCLC, page 22:"}, {"text": "A great deal of excellent humour was expended on the perplexities of mine host.", "reference": "1824, Geoffrey Crayon [pseudonym; Washington Irving], Tales of a Traveller, (please specify |part=1 to 4), Philadelphia, Pa.: H[enry] C[harles] Carey & I[saac] Lea, […], →OCLC:"}, {"text": "They stayed together during three dances, went out on to the terrace, explored wherever they were permitted to explore, paid two visits to the buffet, and enjoyed themselves much in the same way as if they had been school-children surreptitiously breaking loose from an assembly of grown-ups. The boy became volubly friendly and bubbling over with unexpected humour and high spirits.", "reference": "1909 September 9, Archibald Marshall [pseudonym; Arthur Hammond Marshall], “A Court Ball”, in The Squire’s Daughter, London: Methuen & Co. […], →OCLC, page 9:"}, {"text": "Charles had not been employed above six months at Darracott Place, but he was not such a whopstraw as to make the least noise in the performance of his duties when his lordship was out of humour.", "reference": "1959, Georgette Heyer, chapter 1, in The Unknown Ajax:"}], "synonyms": ["amusingness", "comedy", "comicality", "wit"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(uncountable) A mood, especially a bad mood; a temporary state of mind or disposition brought upon by an event; an abrupt illogical inclination or whim.", "tags": ["UK", "uncountable", "usually"], "examples": ["He was in a particularly vile humour that afternoon."], "quotes": [{"text": "I like not the humour of lying.", "reference": "c. 1597 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Merry Wiues of Windsor”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act II, scene i]:"}, {"text": "King James, as he was a Prince of great Judgment, ſo he was a Prince of a marvellous pleaſant humour; […]", "reference": "a. 1627 (date written), Francis Bacon, “[Baconiana Politico-Moralia. […].] Certain Apothegms of the Lord Bacon’s, hitherto Unpublished. [Apophthegm 10.]”, in [Thomas Tenison], editor, Baconiana. Or Certain Genuine Remains of Sr. Francis Bacon, […], London: […] J. D. for Richard Chiswell, […], published 1679, →OCLC, page 55:"}, {"text": "Examine how your humour is inclined, / And which the ruling passion of your mind.", "reference": "1684, Lord Roscommon, Essay on Translated Verse:"}, {"text": "Is my friend all perfection, all virtue and discretion? Has he not humours to be endured?", "reference": "1692–1717, Robert South, Twelve Sermons Preached upon Several Occasions, volume (please specify |volume=I to VI), London:"}, {"text": "([I]t was the town's humour to be always gassing of phantom investors who were likely to come any moment and pay a thousand prices for everything)—“[…]Them rich fellers, they don't make no bad breaks with their money.[…]”", "reference": "1899, Stephen Crane, chapter 1, in Twelve O'Clock:"}], "synonyms": ["mood"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "ar", "name": "Arabic"}, "word": "مِزَاج"}, {"language": {"code": "be", "name": "Belarusian"}, "word": "настро́й"}, {"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "настрое́ние"}, {"language": {"code": "ca", "name": "Catalan"}, "word": "humor"}, {"language": {"code": "cmn", "name": "Chinese Mandarin"}, "word": "心情"}, {"language": {"code": "cs", "name": "Czech"}, "word": "nálada"}, {"language": {"code": "da", "name": "Danish"}, "word": "humør"}, {"language": {"code": "nl", "name": "Dutch"}, "word": "stemming"}, {"language": {"code": "nl", "name": "Dutch"}, "word": "humeur"}, {"language": {"code": "eo", "name": "Esperanto"}, "word": "humoro"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "tuuli"}, {"language": {"code": "fr", "name": "French"}, "word": "humeur"}, {"language": {"code": "fr", "name": "French"}, "word": "disposition"}, {"language": {"code": "de", "name": "German"}, "word": "Laune"}, {"language": {"code": "de", "name": "German"}, "word": "Humor"}, {"language": {"code": "de", "name": "German"}, "word": "Stimmung"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "kedv"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "hangulat"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "humor"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "kedély"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "kedélyállapot"}, {"language": {"code": "is", "name": "Icelandic"}, "word": "skap"}, {"language": {"code": "ia", "name": "Interlingua"}, "w